WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.170
- A week before the pandemic,

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:03.900
just as the world was shutting down,

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.340
my next guest released
her memoir, "Untamed."

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.500
Its message, that we can do hard things,

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.080
came at the perfect time for a lot of us.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.130
What?!

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.520
To date, it sold over 2 million copies

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.400
and spent 65 straight
weeks on the New York Times

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.260
best-seller list.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.560
So, what?!

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.393
I read it.

00:00:21.393 --> 00:00:24.000
I not only read it, I'm like
that nerd that, literally,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.320
I had a necklace made
of a fricking cheetah.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.600
I'm not kidding.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.810
I'm so in.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.940
I'm one of those people
that she's gonna run from

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:31.773
at, like, events.

00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:33.360
Like, lectures or something.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.230
But now she has a new project,
which I already know about.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.190
It's a podcast called
"We Can Do Hard Things,"

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.840
and we have her on the line right now.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.021
So, everybody, give it
up for Glennon Doyle!

00:00:44.021 --> 00:00:48.124
(upbeat music)
(audience applauds)

00:00:48.124 --> 00:00:48.957
(air whooshes)

00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:51.810
I literally, I was like, she's
gonna either think it's cute

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.500
or be like stage five clinger.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.333
I did.

00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:56.960
I was like, you know what,
I just want a symbol.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.200
I just wanna see it.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.040
'Cause I was like, "A tattoo was a lot."

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.610
So I was like, "I'm just gonna makes,"

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.170
you know, that's, I dunno, it's
permanent and I have a lot.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.800
So, I got a necklace made.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.293
I'm not creepy, I promise.

00:01:07.293 --> 00:01:09.027
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:09.027 --> 00:01:09.860
- I'm loving it.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.040
I love it so much.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.610
- So, you, you typically
bring people together,

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.010
but you and your wife, Abby,

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.520
set off a fierce online
debate regarding dishwashers.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.730
- Yes, she is so controlling
about the dishwasher, Kelly.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.740
Sometimes I load it

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.180
and she will reload it
when I leave the room.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.530
So it's more organized.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.930
I don't know what people's
problem is about doing that.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:34.780
I'm not sure.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.300
- Well, just to let you know,

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.420
I have found her
doppelganger and that is me.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.793
I am Abby.
(everybody laughs)

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.729
I am the one, I'm like, "Just stop."

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:49.610
(Kelly laughs)
Like, "I'll do it."

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.660
But we have the video
that kicked this off.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:52.870
It's awesome.
(air whooshes)

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:54.552
- [Abby] This is a kerfuffle.

00:01:54.552 --> 00:01:57.523
- [Glennon] What are you talking about?

00:01:57.523 --> 00:02:01.570
- [Abby] Glennon, this is not
how you pack the dishwasher.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.005
- [Tish] It's gonna wash the same.

00:02:03.005 --> 00:02:04.840
- [Glennon] Exactly, Tish.
- [Abby] No, no, no, no, no.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.103
Here's the problem.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.250
When I redo this, it
wouldn't even need a wash.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.390
We could wait a whole day.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.223
- [Glennon] You know what?

00:02:13.223 --> 00:02:14.640
You, oh, you're gonna redo this.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.111
You are unbelievably dish controlling.

00:02:18.111 --> 00:02:19.343
(air whooshes)
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:19.343 --> 00:02:21.660
(audience applauds)
- "I don't know, Glennon."

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:22.822
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:22.822 --> 00:02:25.037
I saw that, and I was like-

00:02:25.037 --> 00:02:26.715
- Untamed dishwasher.

00:02:26.715 --> 00:02:28.370
It was an untamed dishwasher.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.950
- I, it, well, something should be tamed,

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.640
is what I'm saying, because I saw that

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:33.960
and I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.750
And if there's food on it, I can't."

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.200
Like, I don't think you
need to like wash it.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.840
But, like, there's not tiny
people in the dishwasher,

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.910
like, scrubbing it for you, you know?

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:42.750
So you kind of do it like...

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.470
I am 100% Abby on this one.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.680
So, you have lost me in this.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.370
But, I wanna say, it's nice
though, to have a partner,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.530
like, in life that will
do the dishes with you.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:54.900
I'll say that.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.740
But, Abby, Abby is a gold
medalist and World Cup champion,

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.140
and now she's coaching your daughter.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.400
That's so cool.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.650
- I know.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.840
She was coaching just a
couple of weekends ago

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.580
and they were going into penalty kicks.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.990
And I walked over to her
and I said, "Are you good?"

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:09.550
And she looked very cool on the outside

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.410
and she said, "I've
never been this nervous.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:14.230
Not in the World Cup,
not in the Olympics."

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:19.230
She was terrified, but she's
an amazing coach to the girls.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.110
- Oh, that's so, what a cool thing

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.880
to just have in your
house, like to alert us.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.100
Wow, it's impressive!

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.640
And speaking of your daughter,
I absolutely love this story.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.170
She wrote and sang the
theme song for your podcast.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.384
And I wanna show a little bit of it.

00:03:32.384 --> 00:03:33.217
(air whooshes)

00:03:33.217 --> 00:03:38.217
♪ 'Cause we're adventurous
and heartbreak's our map ♪

00:03:38.662 --> 00:03:43.605
♪ A final destination we lack ♪

00:03:43.605 --> 00:03:48.485
♪ We've stopped asking directions ♪

00:03:48.485 --> 00:03:52.760
♪ To places they've never been ♪

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:53.593
(air whooshes)
(audience applauds)

00:03:53.593 --> 00:03:55.703
You better go ahead with that vibrato.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.030
I, was this her idea?

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.090
- Completely her idea.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.670
She wrote the lyrics on the
way to soccer practice one day.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.020
She is going to absolutely die,

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.950
when she sees that you are
talking about her song right now

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.200
She's gonna die.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.530
- Oh, my gosh, I was so impressed.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.410
- Oh, thank you, thank you.
- Yeah!

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.240
So how did it all come about, is it,

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.850
I heard that Brandi was
involved, Brandi Carlisle.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.500
- Yeah, so, Brandi is a
dear friend of our family.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.340
Tish wrote this little song.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.497
I sent it to Brandi and Brandi said,

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:26.630
"Glennon, this is a really good song."

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.420
And then she sent it back
to us, all produced up.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.920
And she sang background for Tish.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.880
And it's just, Tish is such
a sensitive little girl,

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.280
and it's so wonderful
to have her find music

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.970
as a way to express
herself and show her heart.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:42.760
It's been, just absolutely wonderful.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.740
- 100%, I mean, the arts are for empaths.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.520
Like, I think that's
where you feel everything

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.600
and you express everything.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.450
I, 100%.

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.470
So, this podcast though,
is a family affair.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:53.470
So, can you tell us about it?

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:55.120
Everyone's kind of involved, right?

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.660
- Well, I mean, we
started it during COVID,

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.760
so, we weren't, like,
allowed to be with anyone

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.400
but the family.
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.610
- Those were your options.
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.230
- My options were just sitting
at my kitchen table, right?

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.328
We had to work with what we had.

00:05:07.328 --> 00:05:11.480
But, I really, I've started
recording it with my sister

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.250
and my sister has been
my, I call her my lobster.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:17.410
She's been my person since
I was four years old.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.780
And we've spent our entire
life talking about hard things

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.820
and healing from everything together.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:26.010
And so now, I get to, she's
just like the best thing I have

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.740
and now I get to give her to
the world through this podcast.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.370
And it's just been such a joy.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:32.430
- Oh, I have a sister like that as well.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:33.810
My older sister is also, well,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.370
and nobody ever thinks
she's my older sister.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:36.810
She'll love that I said that.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:39.740
But she's my lobster as well.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:43.160
- So interesting because
I think that sometimes

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.320
we make the mistake of
thinking, "Okay, it's my job."

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.310
And especially for women,
"Okay, it's my job to make sure

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:51.730
that everybody on my island loves

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:54.387
and respects and accepts
me and my family."

00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:01.270
And I think, actually,
it's like, "No, our job

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:06.280
and joy is to only allow
people on our island

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:11.280
who already do respect and
love and celebrate us."

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.490
(audience applauds)
(air whooshes)

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.810
- Amen, those that are deserving.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.210
I'm back with bestselling
author and activist,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.206
Glennon Doyle, y'all give it up for her.

00:06:20.206 --> 00:06:21.680
(Kelly applauds)

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.927
That was a clip from her new podcast,

00:06:23.927 --> 00:06:26.270
"We Can Do Hard Things."

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:29.880
The pandemic, though, it
upended life, as we know it.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.640
And as the world gets
back to somewhat normal,

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.410
Glennon has advice for how we
can navigate this transition.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.260
And I'll take all the advice

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.960
and make all the necklaces about it.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:41.380
Well, some of our audience members

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:42.660
have questions for Glennon.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.080
Malane, from Seattle, what's
your question for Glennon?

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.270
- So, you guys all have multiple kids.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:52.960
We have three boys, ages, 16, 13, and 12,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.950
and each one of them is
navigating this back to normal

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:56.860
so differently.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:00.010
Our oldest is probably
my, our most introverted,

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.380
but he's got the baseball and
the sports and that's helping.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.340
Our middle child is our free spirit,

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.590
has a nice group of friends.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.840
He's smack in the middle of middle school.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:10.080
So he's doing pretty well.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.690
Our youngest has withdrawn during all this

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:14.340
and he's our Tish.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.620
Like, he's the sweet sensitive
one that always has had

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:19.710
a lot of friends and has
been great with adults.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:23.070
And so, I'm just wondering
what suggestions do you have

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:27.700
for how to navigate this
with them individually

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:29.750
and give them the support that they need?

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.530
- Okay, Malane.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.380
Here's how I feel about these little ones.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:37.040
I just don't want us to forget

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.150
that we have all been
through a huge trauma, right?

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:43.680
None of, we're just like all
expecting to go back to normal,

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:44.960
everyone should be normal.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.980
But we've all been through so much.

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:49.580
And these kids have experienced stuff

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.260
that we've never experienced, right?

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:55.560
And our sensitive ones
have been feeling it all.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.560
So they might have the
longest process of recovering

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:02.970
and going back to normal, right?

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.530
But remember what we talk
about over and over, Malane,

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.720
that being human is not about
feeling happy all the time.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.200
It's about feeling everything, right?

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.970
So we just be, we just
be so relentlessly tender

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:18.630
and gentle with these little ones.

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:21.260
And we let them to recover

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.290
at whatever pace they are recovering.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:25.080
And we don't rush them

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.090
and we don't push them back into normalcy.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:29.540
We just allow them to feel it all.

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:32.610
And we let them see us feel it all, right?

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:35.340
We let them see us a
little anxious and scared.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:38.630
And, when they, the more
fully human they see us being,

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.660
the more fully human they
will allow themselves to be.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:42.780
- Thank you.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.430
- But, thinking about that,
no, it is, it is funny.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:46.830
I don't know if this
is a good or bad thing

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.830
and maybe you can answer,
or give, you know,

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:50.870
enlighten me with what you think, Glennon.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:54.230
But I, my daughter feels everything.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:57.060
How you describe your
daughter, is 100% River.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.390
And she feels everything in the room.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:01.410
She takes it all on everyone's emotion.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.230
She can't, she's a walking little me.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.970
But is it good or bad,
do you think to say,

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:07.380
'cause even last night, like,

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:08.607
we were talking about
something and I was like,

00:09:08.607 --> 00:09:10.960
"Okay, it's okay to be
frustrated and angry.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:12.340
Totally okay."

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:13.610
I just don't, I don't think it's good,

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:15.170
maybe if we sit in it all night.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:16.840
Like, let's talk about it and let's do it,

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:18.340
but let's not sit in it.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:19.790
I was like, "We can have a moment

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:21.010
if you need to do it every day

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:23.020
and you need to talk
about this because we're,"

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:24.690
you know, our family is
going through a divorce.

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:25.970
So, it's like, "We can talk about it.

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:28.320
Let's go, let's, I wanna go
there, but I don't wanna sit

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:29.490
in this like emotional day."

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:30.950
Is that bad or not?

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:32.070
Or, I don't know

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:33.620
- Kelly, I have the exact same,

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:35.770
I have this theory that
we just pendulum parents.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:38.600
So, I felt like my parents
didn't give me enough space

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:39.570
to feel my feelings.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:41.160
So then I accidentally taught my kids

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.590
that they should feel all
their feelings all the time.

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:46.840
And, sometimes, I'm watching
Tish talking about her feelings

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:47.720
for the second hour.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:49.680
And I'm like, "You know
what, I did you wrong."

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:50.513
(Kelly laughs)

00:09:50.513 --> 00:09:52.487
"I should have taught you
to suffer silently," right?

00:09:52.487 --> 00:09:57.487
"'Cause this is," oh, we just overdo it.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.810
And, Kelly, I said several times,

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.157
lots of times to these kids,

00:10:02.157 --> 00:10:03.840
"You may continue feeling your feelings.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.110
I just need you to do it in
your room, away from me."

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.628
Just us parents.
(Kelly laughs)

00:10:09.628 --> 00:10:10.461
- Oh, my God.

00:10:10.461 --> 00:10:11.990
That was amazing.

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:12.823
And thank you, Malane.

00:10:12.823 --> 00:10:13.670
What a great question.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.780
A lot of us parents are,
are struggling with that.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.330
All our kids are experiencing,
not just the pandemic,

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:20.910
but you know, the previous administration

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:22.700
whatever side of the fence
you are, it was very intense.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:24.720
And it was constantly,
something happening.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.160
It's been a rough few years.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.230
Like, no matter what side
of the fence you're on,

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:29.770
it's been hard for kids and adults.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.170
So, like you said, there's
been a lot of trauma.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:35.650
And, everybody, "We Can Do
Hard Things" with Glennon Doyle

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:37.500
is available wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:39.920
Do yourself a favor and listen.

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.020
And don't forget to pick up "Untamed."

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:43.800
I promise you'll love it.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:45.600
And if you don't, then you're wrong.

